# Plum-Audio unit table — one line per unit, consumed by deploy.sh.
#
# COPY THIS FILE to `units.conf` (which is gitignored) and put your own units in it. The addresses
# below are RFC 5737 documentation addresses and route nowhere; they are shaped like a real two-VLAN
# rig only to show what the columns mean.
#
#   host | unit id | unit name | player id | player name | DAC device
#
# The ids are NOT cosmetic: the mesh keys routing, group membership and per-player volume off them,
# and settings.json on each unit already refers to them. They match what the pre-container
# ~/plum-test stack ran with, so a unit that moves into the container keeps its identity and its
# existing routes rather than reappearing as a stranger.
#
# KEEP EVERY COLUMN UNIQUE ACROSS ROWS. A clash does not stop a deploy — deploy.sh appends a stable
# per-unit token (the Pi's SoC serial, last four hex) to whichever field is duplicated and warns, so
# `Kitchen` twice becomes `Kitchen-EE12` and `Kitchen-A17B`. That is a fallback, not a feature: the names
# it produces are nobody's choice, and the ids are what the mesh routes on.
#
# The unit-name column is the one whose reach is easy to underestimate. deploy.sh writes it as
# PLUM_UNIT_NAME, and a unit with no settings.json yet adopts it BOTH as its own display name and as
# the name of every source endpoint it offers. Two rows sharing a name therefore give two units that
# are indistinguishable in the mesh view, in the GUI's unit cards, and to an AirPlay sender choosing
# a speaker. Renaming a unit later, in Settings, overrides it permanently on that unit — this column
# only decides what an unnamed unit boots as, and is NOT re-applied by a later deploy.
#
# 192.0.2.x below is a two-unit mesh rig; 198.51.100.x sits on a second VLAN alongside a third-party
# Sendspin server (Music Assistant) — interop can only be tested on the same segment, because mDNS is
# link-local. See tests/Integration/README.md.
#
# The DAC column is matched by PortAudio as a SUBSTRING of its own device name, not handed to ALSA
# — so it must appear in `sd.query_devices()`, which is not the same list as `aplay -l`. Prefer the
# card's long name over an `hw:C,D` id: card NUMBERS move (on .100.21 the HAT is card 2, behind two
# vc4hdmi cards), the long name does not. `bcm2835` and `snd_rpi_hifiberry_dacplus` are both
# verified to open at 44100/2/16.
#
# `none` is the ONE value that is not a PortAudio fragment. It means this host has no audio output:
# deploy.sh selects the headless compose profile (no /dev/snd — the audio profile cannot even be
# CREATED without it), writes PLUM_PLAYER_ENABLED=0, and expects three supervisord programs instead
# of four. Such a unit ingests AirPlay/Spotify and routes to other rooms; it renders nothing itself
# and advertises no _sendspin._tcp speaker. The player id and name columns are still required — they
# are the row's shape — but unused.

192.0.2.10     | unit-kitchen | Kitchen     | player-kitchen | Kitchen Speaker | bcm2835
192.0.2.11     | unit-living  | Living Room | player-living  | Living Room     | snd_rpi_hifiberry_dacplus
198.51.100.20  | unit-office  | Office      | player-office  | Office Speaker  | bcm2835

# A unit with no sound card at all: it ingests AirPlay/Spotify/Bluetooth and routes to other rooms,
# but renders nothing itself. The player columns are still required — they are the row's shape.
# 198.51.100.21  | unit-hall | Hallway | player-hall | Hallway | none
